ABOUTME: quiche patch applied by build-libquiche.sh: bound the fragments a stream buffers out of order
ABOUTME: (cloudflare/quiche#2814; upstreamable as is, the diff starts below).

Bound the out-of-order fragments a stream buffers

RecvBuf keeps out-of-order stream data in one map entry per fragment,
each costing about 100-150 bytes beyond its data. Fragments only count
against flow control by the offsets they span, so a peer sending one-byte
STREAM frames one byte apart, with the stream's first byte withheld, makes
a receiver hold about 75 times its flow-control window, until the stream
or connection goes: about 38 MiB for a 512 KiB connection window, from
2 MiB of STREAM frames, measured with a counting allocator.

Allow a stream MIN_FRAGMENTS (64) fragments, plus one per
MIN_AVERAGE_FRAGMENT_LEN (256) bytes of the stream they span, gaps
included; past that, a write fails with Error::FlowControl, closing the
connection with FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR (the peer used more receive buffer than
the advertised limits allow). Full-sized packets carry more than 1000
bytes of stream data each, so a window buffered after any pattern of loss
stays well within the bound. Past MIN_FRAGMENTS, small fragments that
continue the previous one while earlier data is missing are coalesced
into it, up to 2048 bytes, so a sender writing little at a time isn't
refused after a loss.

A stream's out-of-order buffer then costs at most its data plus
(64 + span / 256) fragments, so a connection's at most its window plus
(64 * streams + window / 256) fragments.

diff --git a/quiche/src/stream/recv_buf.rs b/quiche/src/stream/recv_buf.rs
index 738f2292..454073eb 100644
--- a/quiche/src/stream/recv_buf.rs
+++ b/quiche/src/stream/recv_buf.rs
@@ -41,6 +41,29 @@ use crate::flowcontrol;
 
 use crate::range_buf::RangeBuf;
 
+/// Fragments a stream buffers whatever their size.
+///
+/// Out-of-order data is buffered in fragments that each cost memory beyond
+/// their bytes. Past this many, a stream may buffer one more fragment per
+/// `MIN_AVERAGE_FRAGMENT_LEN` bytes of the stream it spans (gaps included),
+/// so a peer sending tiny fragments separated by gaps can't make the
+/// buffer cost much more than its flow-control window.
+const MIN_FRAGMENTS: usize = 64;
+
+/// Stream bytes per fragment allowed past `MIN_FRAGMENTS`.
+///
+/// Frames of a full-sized packet carry more than 1000 bytes, so a window
+/// buffered after losses stays well within this.
+const MIN_AVERAGE_FRAGMENT_LEN: usize = 256;
+
+/// Largest fragment small contiguous fragments are coalesced into.
+///
+/// Past `MIN_FRAGMENTS`, small frames continuing the previous fragment
+/// while earlier data is missing (a sender writing little at a time, after
+/// a loss) are copied into it up to this size, so they don't count against
+/// `MIN_AVERAGE_FRAGMENT_LEN`.
+const MAX_COALESCED_LEN: usize = 2048;
+
 /// Receive-side stream buffer.
 ///
 /// Stream data received by the peer is buffered in a list of data chunks
@@ -188,16 +211,52 @@ impl RecvBuf {
             self.len = cmp::max(self.len, buf.max_off());
 
             if !self.drain {
-                self.data.insert(buf.max_off(), buf);
+                self.insert(buf);
             } else {
                 // we are not storing any data, off == len
                 self.off = self.len;
             }
         }
 
+        // Bound the memory fragments cost beyond their bytes.
+        let span = (self.len - self.off) as usize;
+        if self.data.len() > MIN_FRAGMENTS + span / MIN_AVERAGE_FRAGMENT_LEN {
+            return Err(Error::FlowControl);
+        }
+
         Ok(())
     }
 
+    /// Stores a buffer that doesn't overlap buffered data. Once the stream
+    /// holds `MIN_FRAGMENTS` fragments while earlier data is missing (nothing
+    /// can be read), it is coalesced into the previous fragment when both
+    /// are small and contiguous.
+    fn insert(&mut self, buf: RangeBuf) {
+        if self.data.len() >= MIN_FRAGMENTS &&
+            !buf.is_empty() &&
+            buf.len() < MAX_COALESCED_LEN / 2 &&
+            !self.ready()
+        {
+            // Fragments are keyed by their end offset.
+            if let Some(prev) = self.data.get(&buf.off()) {
+                if !prev.fin() && prev.len() + buf.len() <= MAX_COALESCED_LEN {
+                    let prev = self.data.remove(&buf.off()).unwrap();
+
+                    let mut data = Vec::with_capacity(prev.len() + buf.len());
+                    data.extend_from_slice(&prev);
+                    data.extend_from_slice(&buf);
+
+                    let merged = RangeBuf::from(&data, prev.off(), buf.fin());
+                    self.data.insert(merged.max_off(), merged);
+
+                    return;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+
+        self.data.insert(buf.max_off(), buf);
+    }
+
